@CameronNeylon @COKIproject @MsPhelps @CrossrefOrg @unpaywall Nice. We did a less ambitious but similar thing here based on samples:

https://t.co/sFhSgXTdVT

I broke it down by backfiles and current, showing a slight increase in recent non-English publications.

@Andy_Tattersall @CrossrefOrg And to add to what has already been mentioned:

https://t.co/SyKLdFMBeh:&lt;pmid&gt;

For example:

https://t.co/6LvGKh1q9y

In general- look at the relation filters at https://t.co/FWLWebUo9T

In case you missed it- @CrossrefOrg R&amp;D lives! 

&lt;cue maniacal laugh&gt;

https://t.co/VvrBgtSwMu https://t.co/JZI1rDKGtq

@Knoblauchkeks @CrossrefOrg Is there a way to even do this in PDF that works across PDF viewers?

Also- have updated page (in staging)  with advice to use `pdfwrite -dFastWebView` instead of `pdfopt`. I note however that linearization itself seems deprecated these days.

Tnx.

@franknorman @CrossrefOrg We agree. But note we do support “relationships”, an underused, under-promoted (&amp; possibly inadequate) mechanism  for capturing acknowledgement info. https://t.co/DPLYG2exnw

Also- we are soliciting feedback on our schema:

https://t.co/3i1HHtwbMu

@peder_isager @CrossrefSupport @CrossrefOrg @OSFramework Hi. See Metadata License section here:

https://t.co/8Nhzxl9MxX

We consider the metadata to be facts. So go ahead. But acknowledgement would be nice ;-).

@valexiev1 @opencitations @CrossrefOrg @figshare @i4oc_org Try using content negotiation on the DOI as per: https://t.co/w6dyBHpTYF

Or direct API:

https://t.co/DL3T6lOS3v

@csarven @CrossrefOrg But any including link to actual research object in metadata. See link header here:curl -X HEAD \
  https://t.co/WAokdSJgF5 \
  -H 'Accept: text/turtle'
